## Setup

### 1. Get the material

```bash
git clone https://github.com/<your-username>/<repo-name>.git training
cd training
mkdir -p raw_data   # only needed if the folder is missing
```

### 2. Install the command-line tools

```bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt install fastqc cutadapt trim-galore rna-star multiqc
```

If `multiqc` is not available through `apt`, install it with `pip install multiqc`. Check that the tools work:

```bash
fastqc --version && cutadapt --version && trim_galore --version && STAR --version && multiqc --version
```

### 3. Install the R packages

In R or RStudio:

```r
install.packages(c("tidyverse", "BiocManager", "pheatmap"))
BiocManager::install(c("rtracklayer", "DESeq2", "EnhancedVolcano"))
```

### 4. Set your folder in the R scripts

The two R scripts start with this line. Change it to wherever your `training/` folder is:

```r
training_dir <- path.expand("~/Desktop/bioinformatics_workshop/training")
```

### 5. Match the number of CPU cores (optional)

The scripts use 4 threads for FastQC and Trim Galore and 8 for STAR (`THREADS=8` at the top of `ecoli_gene_quantification.sh`). Check how many cores you have with `nproc`, and lower these numbers if you have fewer.

