## Step 3 – Quality control

```bash
bash scripts/fast_qc.sh
```

**FastQC** writes a quality report for every FASTQ file into `raw_data/fastqc_results/`. **MultiQC** combines them into one page, `raw_data/fastqc_results/multiqc_report/multiqc_report.html`. Open it in a web browser.

| FastQC section | What a good result looks like |
|---|---|
| Per base sequence quality | Most bases above Phred 30. A drop at the ends of reads means trimming is needed. |
| Per sequence quality scores | Most reads have a high average quality. |
| Per base sequence content | Some bias in the first few bases is normal; it should even out after trimming. |
| Adapter content | No adapters. If adapters are present, they must be removed before alignment. |
| Per sequence GC content | One peak near the GC content of *E. coli* (about 51 %). Extra peaks can mean contamination. |
| Sequence duplication levels | Low to moderate. High duplication can mean PCR bias or over-sequencing. |

---

## Step 4 – Trimming

```bash
bash scripts/trim.sh
```

**Trim Galore** removes adapters and low-quality bases from the ends of the reads. With the settings used here it also:

- keeps the two reads of each pair together (`--paired`);
- discards pairs where a read is shorter than 36 bases after trimming (`--length 36`);
- runs FastQC on the trimmed reads (`--fastqc`), so you can compare before and after.

Samples whose FASTQ files are missing are skipped with a message, so finish Step 1 first.

For each sample, `raw_data/trimmed_reads/` gets:

| File | Content |
|---|---|
| `<sample>_1_val_1.fq.gz`, `<sample>_2_val_2.fq.gz` | Trimmed reads (used in Step 5) |
| `*_trimming_report.txt` | How many adapters and bases were removed |
| `*_val_*_fastqc.html` | FastQC report of the trimmed reads |

