## Step 5 – Indexing, alignment and gene counting

```bash
bash scripts/ecoli_gene_quantification.sh
```

**Indexing.** An aligner cannot compare every read with the whole genome base by base; that would be far too slow. STAR first builds an **index** of the genome, which lets it find where each read belongs very quickly. The index is built once into `raw_data/star_index/` and reused for every sample; if it already exists, the script skips this step.

**Alignment and counting.** STAR then aligns each pair of trimmed reads. With `--quantMode GeneCounts` it also uses the GTF to count how many reads fall on each gene, so alignment and counting happen in one step.

| Option | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `--runThreadN 8` | Number of CPU cores (set by `THREADS` at the top of the script). |
| `--sjdbOverhang 149` | Read length minus 1 (the reads are 150 bases). |
| `--genomeSAindexNbases 10` | Needed for small genomes like *E. coli*. Without it, STAR can run out of memory or crash. |
| `--readFilesCommand zcat` | Lets STAR read the compressed `.fq.gz` files directly. |
| `--outSAMtype BAM SortedByCoordinate` | Writes a sorted BAM file. |
| `--quantMode GeneCounts` | Writes the gene counts (`ReadsPerGene.out.tab`). |

Each sample gets these files in `raw_data/star_align/`:

| File | Content |
|---|---|
| `<sample>_Aligned.sortedByCoord.out.bam` | Aligned reads |
| `<sample>_ReadsPerGene.out.tab` | Reads counted per gene (used in Step 6) |
| `<sample>_Log.final.out` | Mapping summary |

At the end, the script prints the mapping rate of every sample (the `Uniquely mapped reads %` line of each `Log.final.out`). The paper reports about **79–85 %** of reads mapped.

### Understanding `ReadsPerGene.out.tab`

Each `ReadsPerGene.out.tab` file has four columns:

| Column | Content | Use it when the library is… |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Gene ID (for example `b0204`) | — |
| 2 | Counts ignoring strand | unstranded |
| 3 | Counts for reads on the same strand as the gene | forward-stranded |
| 4 | Counts for reads on the opposite strand to the gene | reverse-stranded |

The first four rows (`N_unmapped`, `N_multimapping`, `N_noFeature`, `N_ambiguous`) are summary numbers, not genes.

<p align="center">
  <img src="Document/images/readspergene_example.png" width="380" alt="First rows of a ReadsPerGene.out.tab file">
  <br><em>The first rows of a ReadsPerGene.out.tab file from this dataset.</em>
</p>

**Which column to use?** Compare columns 3 and 4: if they are similar, the library is unstranded (column 2). If column 3 is much larger, it is forward-stranded (column 3). If column 4 is much larger, it is reverse-stranded (column 4).

> **In this dataset, use column 4.** Column 3 is close to zero and column 4 is almost equal to column 2 (gene `b0204`: 50188, 33, 50158). The library is **reverse-stranded**.

Check a whole file at once:

```bash
tail -n +5 raw_data/star_align/SRR13970433_ReadsPerGene.out.tab | \
  awk '{c2+=$2; c3+=$3; c4+=$4} END {print "col2:", c2, " col3:", c3, " col4:", c4}'
```

